using the python debugger in a terminal  in this video we'll learn how to use the  python debugger in the terminal  for this example  we're running code from the token  classification section  downloading the cornell dataset before  loading a tokenizer to preprocess it  check out the section of the course  linked below for more information  once this is done we try to batch  together some features of the training  data set by padding them and returning a  tensor  if we try to execute our scripts in a  terminal we get an error  note that we use pi torch here with  redundant source equal pt but you would  get the same error with tensorflow  as we have seen in the how to debug in  our video there are messages at the end  and it indicates we should use padding  which we're actually trying to do so  this is not useful and we need to go a  little deeper to debug the problem  fortunately you can use the python  debugger quite easily in your terminal  by launching your script with python  minus m pdb  and then the name of the training script  when executing that command you are sent  to the first instruction of your script  you can run just the next instruction by  tapping n and enter  or you can continue directly to zero by  typing c  and enter  once there you go to the very bottom of  the traceback and you can type commands  the first two commands you should learn  are u and d for up and down  this allows you to get up and down in  the traceback  going up twice we get to the point the  error was reached  the first command to learn is p for  print it allows you to print any value  you want for instance here we can see  the value of written tensors  or batch outputs to try to understand  what triggered the error  the batch outputs dictionary is a bit  hard to see so let's dive into smaller  pieces of it  inside the debugger you can not only  print any variable but also evaluate any  expression so we can look independently  at the inputs  or the labels  those labels are definitely weird  they are various size which we can  confirm by printing the sites using a  list comprehension  no wonder the token either wasn't able  to create an answer with them  this is because the pad method only  takes care of the tokenizer outputs the  input ids the attention mask and the  token type ids  so we have to patch the label ourselves  before trying to create a new tensor  with them  once you're ready to exit the python  debugger you can press q for quit and  enter another way we can access the  python debugger  is to put a breaking point in our script  we can do this using the  pdb.settrace method  as long as we import the pdb module at  the beginning of our script  saving  and then relaunching our script  with just python  we'll stop the execution at the breaking  point we set  we can inspect all the variables before  the next transaction is executed again  for instance here the features  typing n and enter execute the next  instruction which takes us back inside  traceback  one way to fix zero manually is to pad  all the labels to the longest  another way is to use the data creator  suitable for token classification  if you want to learn how to use the  python debugger in a notebook check out  the video link below
 

